WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:13.070
And so today, we're going to talk about what
happens if and when intelligent life contacts

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.800
us. So what would that be like, what should
we say, will we understand what they're saying?

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:23.981
So to help us discuss this, our first guest
is an anthropologist at York University. And

00:00:23.981 --> 00:00:28.730
she researches, amongst many other things,
the evolution of intelligence, the colonization

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:34.550
of space. She's collaborated with The NASA
Astrobiology Institute, the SETI Institute,

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:41.460
and the 100-Year Starship Program. Please
welcome Kathryn Denning!

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:49.460
Our next guest is an astronomer at Columbia
University, where he leads the Cool Worlds

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:55.690
lab. His research team is focused on discovering
new planets and moons. Please welcome David

00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:03.290
Kipping!
Also joining us, we have a philosopher from

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:09.500
Barnard College, which is at Columbia University.
She specializes in the philosophy of language,

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.829
and is the co-founder and co-organizer of
PhLip, an annual conference that brings together

00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:23.749
philosophers and linguists from all over the
world. Please welcome Karen Lewis!

00:01:23.749 --> 00:01:28.970
And our final guest is a biophysicist, and
head of the Laboratory of Integrative Neuroscience

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:34.590
at Rockefeller University. Among many things,
he studies the brains and communication skills

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:40.579
of dolphins, which is kind of like an alien
species. So he's an expert in deciphering

00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:53.630
alien languages. Please welcome Marcelo Magnasco!
So David, catch us up to speed on the ways

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.079
in which science is currently searching for
intelligent life.

00:01:57.079 --> 00:02:03.189
Sure. So probably the most famous program
that's trying to look for evidence of extraterrestrial

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:07.590
civilizations is called SETI. S. E. T. I.,
that's the search for extraterrestrial intelligence.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:13.050
And traditionally, ever since Frank Drake
first started this project, I think back in

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:17.810
the 1960's with a famous project called Project
Ozma, they started listening for radio signals,

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:22.350
essentially hoping that somebody was transmitting
our direction. And that makes a lot of sense.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:26.050
Radio, which is a form of electromagnetic
radiation, travels at the speed of light.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:30.340
So it's the fastest form of communication,
and it's very cheap to produce and receive.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:35.280
So it kind of makes sense, as a way to look.
More recently, scientists, and I guess you

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.360
might bunch me in with this group, have been
thinking about other ways that you might be

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:43.360
able to reveal the presence of extraterrestrial
civilizations, one of which might be that

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.340
they are not intending to tell us that they
are here. They're not deliberately messaging

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.640
us, but maybe we can still tell that they
are there by virtue of their technologies.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:56.680
Maybe they're building some giant alien engineering
megastructure, and we might be able to see

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.670
them in their act of building that, or maybe
see the waste heat, for instance, from the

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:05.190
giant machines they're using. So recently,
both of those approaches have emerged as sort

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:10.110
of the main avenues we're using.
And how do you see if someone is trying to

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:14.340
build this weird, alien structure, you know,
thousands of miles away?

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.900
Yeah, that's a good question. It's almost
like Arthur C. Clarke would say that such

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.490
an advanced civilization would be indistinguishable
from magic, in a way. So how on Earth can

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.120
you look for something which could essentially
be magic? And that's kind of what, in a way,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.620
we do. We look for things which are unnatural.
So we think we understand how stars work.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.280
We think we understand how planets are built
around planetary systems, and we look for

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.070
stuff that essentially just cannot be explained
by any other means.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:44.360
There's a very famous type of signal that
we look for called a Dyson Sphere, maybe some

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.470
of you have heard of that before. Freeman
Dyson famously proposed that in the 1960s,

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:54.510
and it's essentially saying: well, you know,
if energy growth continues at 2% per year,

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:59.740
which is about how energy growth has been
increasing over the last few decades, eventually

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:04.640
in the year 3500 or something if you extrapolate
it, we would need the energy from the entire

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.600
Sun. So how do you take the entire energy
of a star? You build a shell around it. So

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.240
we could maybe look for these giant shells
built around stars. So you won't see the starlight

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.789
because it's been blocked out, but you would
see all of the waste heat that they were generating.

00:04:18.789 --> 00:04:24.599
So they can't get around the laws of thermodynamics,
hopefully. If they can beat thermodynamics,

00:04:24.599 --> 00:04:30.110
then basically we have no idea about how physics
works at all. And if they have to obey thermodynamics,

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:34.349
then they should emit, still, some waste heat
energy. And we look for the heat signature

00:04:34.349 --> 00:04:38.949
of stars which do not appear in visible light,
but appear in infrared. And that would be

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:43.210
a giveaway that somebody is extracting all
of the light energy, and doing something with

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.919
it. So that's a type of signature that we
can look for.

00:04:45.919 --> 00:04:49.389
Any luck so far?
No, actually, but people have looked. So there's

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:53.110
been about 100 thousand nearby stars which
have been surveyed for Dyson Spheres, and

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:57.479
about 100 thousand galaxies which have been
surveyed. And so far, there's no conclusive

00:04:57.479 --> 00:05:01.629
evidence of such a civilization. Which in
a way, is kind of depressing. It means that

00:05:01.629 --> 00:05:05.960
theses super-advanced civilizations, if they
exist, they don't do that very often. They

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.120
don't use entire stellar outputs of stars.
Yeah.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.759
So we're not just, as a species, in terms
of trying to find alien life, we're not just

00:05:15.759 --> 00:05:20.129
searching for it actively. We're also sending
messages. We're broadcasting. Can you tell

00:05:20.129 --> 00:05:26.090
us a little bit about that?
Sure. So there's an institute called METI

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:31.880
Institute, so Messaging Extraterrestrial Intelligence
Institute. It's an outgrowth of a thread that

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:36.509
was kind of present in SETI from the beginning,
which was the idea that one way to conduct

00:05:36.509 --> 00:05:40.370
the experiment, instead of just listening,
listening, listening, go, “Yoohoo,” and

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:45.090
see if anybody answers.
So there's a pretty major division, I would

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.150
say, within the world of SETI on that subject.
Some people are very firmly of the opinion

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:54.530
that we should just listen, not broadcast.
Others say, “Look. If there's anything out

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:58.270
there to be worried about, chances are it
knows we're here already. So we may as well

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:05.669
say 'hello.'” Really, quite different sets
of opinions on that, and some friction.

00:06:05.669 --> 00:06:09.180
And where do you sit on this? And it's quite
controversial the things that go down.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:14.380
It is quite controversial, and it's related
to the question of risk. I should say, as

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:21.240
an anthropologist rather than a person who
is an astronomer, or somebody composing messages

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:26.170
to transmit, I kind of study the debate. So
I'm going to be annoyingly agnostic on this

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:31.949
one, as well, and not commit. What I like
to do instead is kind of look at this question

00:06:31.949 --> 00:06:37.050
for those who say, in the public in particular,
that we should transmit, why are they saying

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:42.389
that? What are their interests, and what are
their concerns? And for those who say we shouldn't

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:46.210
transmit, what are their reasons? And then
it all kind of spins out into this larger

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:50.189
question about, “Okay, well we all share
this planet together. How do we decide these

00:06:50.189 --> 00:06:56.189
things?” So it kind of spins quickly into
a question of public-scientist relationships,

00:06:56.189 --> 00:07:03.499
and different camps on Earth, and all of that.
So if we do ... Say SETI does pick up a signal,

00:07:03.499 --> 00:07:07.190
David, what kind of signal are we likely to
get?

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:13.189
Well, that's a hard question to answer. It
could be, classically, the signal that people

00:07:13.189 --> 00:07:20.289
have been looking for is just a high-powered,
narrow frequency. Narrow bandpass-range radio

00:07:20.289 --> 00:07:25.800
transmission. But that's all it is. It's just
like a pulse. This star essentially appears

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:29.580
very bright when you look at the specific
wavelength of light, which corresponds to

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:32.119
radio.
Typically, the frequencies they look at is

00:07:32.119 --> 00:07:38.330
something called The Water-Hole. That is a
frequency space in the universe which is naturally

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:44.460
very quiet. So stars produce radio waves,
quasars produce radio waves, galaxies emit

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:48.619
plenty of radio. So you have to choose a frequency
where the universe is not noisy. So there's

00:07:48.619 --> 00:07:53.800
this one particular bandpass called The Water-Hole
that, collectively, SETI scientists have been

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:58.229
sort of honing in on. And if you saw a very
high-powered frequency that was way above

00:07:58.229 --> 00:08:02.240
the cosmic background noise, then you would
say, “Okay, maybe there's something interesting

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.759
here.”
But we've seen those. Actually, SETI@home,

00:08:05.759 --> 00:08:10.979
which is essentially this screensaver you
can download and it processes through data

00:08:10.979 --> 00:08:17.159
that SETI have collected, sees about 72 signals
like that per day, on average. So there are

00:08:17.159 --> 00:08:21.569
72 high-powered frequencies above the background
noise, and most of them are just terrestrial

00:08:21.569 --> 00:08:25.630
interference. So this is just, you know, our
own radio transmitters bouncing off the atmosphere

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:30.469
and coming back down, or satellites in space,
military satellites, even. And it's very difficult

00:08:30.469 --> 00:08:34.460
to go through each one of those and figure
out is this genuinely something alien, or

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:39.300
is this something just that was interfering.
So you know, one of the dreams for a long

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.960
time of SETI has been: what if you could put
a radio telescope on the back of the Moon,

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.150
on the far side of the Moon? You wouldn't
get any of the radio interference from the

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:50.260
Earth whatsoever. So it'd be a very, very
quiet region, and you just have the cosmos

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:55.780
to deal with and no human interference. So
if we could build any kind of radio telescope

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:58.940
for SETI, that'd probably be the radio telescope
that'd we'd want and then maybe we would have

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:05.150
a much clearer view of these things.
So say we did get a signal that wasn't terrestrial

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:09.450
interference, Karen, would we be able to interpret
it? You're the linguist.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:16.810
Okay, well, that's a great question. So I
think there are a lot of challenges in interpreting

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:22.330
a signal. First of all, it's a matter of recognizing
the signal as communicative in the first place,

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:26.230
right? We don't want to try ... I mean, maybe
there's other ways to tell that it's evidence

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:30.120
that there's intelligent life out there, right?
If it's evidence of technology that they couldn't

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:37.350
have if they weren't intelligent. That would
be a non-linguistic way of communicating that

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:41.110
there is intelligent life. But if you think,
“Okay, there's a signal, and it's a piece

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:45.100
of language, and they want to communicate
something,” how do we even figure out that

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:46.950
something's trying to be communicative in
the first place?

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:51.230
So if you think about what a language is,
a language is a symbolic system, first of

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:54.730
all. Meaning, however you encode it, whether
there is sort of sounds that are coming out

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:59.790
of my mouth right now, or a writing system,
or a signing system, or braille, or anything

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:03.210
like that, those are just ... Say the sounds
that are coming out of my mouth, they're just

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:07.900
sounds, but they encode certain meaning. Other
sounds don't encode meaning. And some are

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:11.570
very complex and don't encode meaning. For
example, think of a song, or something like

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:17.380
that. Music is made of notes, but the notes
don't pick out, or refer to a particular thing

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:22.820
in the world. So the first thing is just recognizing
that there's a symbolic system here, rather

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:25.690
than something else that occurs in nature,
something that's not suppose to have meaning

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:29.010
at all.
And second of all, the way language works,

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:35.130
what makes in linguistic is that the speaker
or the sender wanted to communicate something

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:40.060
with that message, and we have to figure what
they wanted to communicate. If it's just this

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:43.480
random noise that came off into the universe,
then we're trying to interpret something that's

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:46.940
not interpretable. So the first thing is just
trying to figure out whether this is something

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:53.020
we should be interpreting in the first place.
The next challenge is, of course, if we think

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:56.961
it is something we should be interpreting,
is how to decode that. And I think there are

00:10:56.961 --> 00:11:03.120
challenges in all sorts of levels. One, we
have to figure out just what the individual

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:08.960
... so every language is made up of building
blocks of meaning. So for example if I just

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:13.300
say something simple like, “This chair is
red and white,” there's the words in a sentence

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:16.170
that are building blocks. There's meaning.
So we want to figure out something like “chair,”

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:20.420
where it refers to this sort of thing.
And then there's the syntax, the grammar,

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:24.660
how they form together. So when I say, “This
chair is red and white,” predicating the

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:28.940
properties of being white and red of the chair,
and we figure that out because we know the

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:32.831
grammar of English. So whatever the communication
we have, if it's anything linguistic, it's

00:11:32.831 --> 00:11:37.920
going to have these building blocks of meaning,
and a way that they go together to communicate

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:41.850
a message. And we're going to have to figure
out both what those building blocks refer

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:49.230
to, and then, how they click together to make
a message. And that's even furthermore going

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:53.390
to be complicated because, chances are, the
aliens have very different things they want

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:58.200
to talk about than we do. So they're not probably
going to be talking about chairs, or grandparents,

00:11:58.200 --> 00:11:59.940
or people.
Internet bubbles.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:06.500
Or internet bubbles, or anything like that.
So even sort of searching for what these building

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:10.640
blocks could refer to is really hard, because
chances are it's going to be sort of outside

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:17.690
of our concepts. And so, even if somehow we
decode that a certain word refers to something,

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:20.424
we have a hypothesis of what sort of thing
it refers to, we're not going to have any

00:12:20.424 --> 00:12:25.540
sense of sort of what role does that thing
play in their society. Just as if we sent

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:29.010
messages about chairs. Even if somehow they
could figure out the word “chair” referred

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:34.500
to this sort of thing, they have no idea what
a chair is really, or what role it plays in

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:39.140
our society. So there's this sort of ... Even
if you can, somehow, and I doubt you can,

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:42.750
get through all those first layers, you're
going to have at the end, even if you somehow

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.990
decode the message in a certain sense, you're
not really going to understand the message.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:51.840
And if I can just add something to that. In
terms of trying to translate these messages,

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:55.790
it maybe seems to us right now like it's a
long way off that we'd be able to do that.

00:12:55.790 --> 00:13:00.310
So maybe the advanced civilization might anticipate
that, and they might not actually attempt

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:04.860
to send, necessarily, a specific message,
but just a broadcast that they are, in the

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.541
first case, just intelligent. So maybe you
could just send a prime number sequence. So

00:13:08.541 --> 00:13:12.300
just a series of, you know, like morse code
almost like, “beep, beep-beep, beep-beep-beep,”

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:17.480
and just send the series of prime numbers.
And then at least we would know that there's

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:21.140
no way that that was a natural process. There's
nothing in nature that just spits out prime

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:26.390
numbers. So you might not be able to necessarily
tell how to build some giant machine like

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:30.960
in Contact, the 1997 film, but you might at
least be able to say, “Hey there's definitely

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:33.980
somebody smart, there, because that shouldn't
happen naturally."

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:38.540
And David, at the moment we're just kind of
... The fun world we live in as we get a signal

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:44.500
from far away, how far away are we talking
here? What is the closest that intelligent

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:48.880
life could currently be based on how much
we've scanned the universe so far?

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:53.580
Yeah, sure. So we do ... I mean, one of the
revolutions of the past 10-20 years in astronomy

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:59.000
has been the discovery of all of these planets
in our solar neighborhood and beyond. The

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:03.260
galaxy is about a hundred thousand light-years
across, to give you some sense of scale, and

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:09.200
we have surveyed fairly well within about,
I'd say, about two thousand light-years of

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:12.990
the Sun looking for planets. And we now know
that most stars have planetary systems around

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:18.480
them, and something like 10% of those stars,
Sun-like stars, have also Earth-like planets

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:23.950
around them. So there is no shortage of Earth-like
planets, some order of tens of billions, likely,

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:29.000
in the Milky Way Galaxy alone.
The nearest example is probably our nearest

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:32.720
star, actually. Proxima Centauri is the nearest
star, you can't actually see it in the sky

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:38.050
because it's too faint. It's a red dwarf star,
much smaller than the Sun. But it is the nearest

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:41.820
star. It's just four light-years away, and
we now know that it has an Earth-like planet

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:46.690
around it. Whether that planet truly is Earth-like,
though, you know, does it actually have a

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:51.030
habitable biosphere around it, does it have
liquid water on it? We don't know. We just

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:55.240
know there's a planet there that is essentially
the same mass as the Earth, and it has, in

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:58.240
principle, the right distance from the star
that it could have liquid water.

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:03.500
Okay, so say that is where intelligent life
is, a mere four light-years away. So if they

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:07.370
had sent us a signal, how long ago would they
have sent that signal?

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:13.290
So if they're continuously broadcasting it,
then we would be able to see it at anytime.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:15.030
All we would have to do is just look up, and
we'd be able to see it.

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:18.040
But when did they make the call?
It would be four years. I mean, if it's four

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:21.230
light-years, then radio, just like light,
travels at the speed of light. So it would

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:26.400
take four years for backwoods communication.
So every text message we would send them,

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.490
there would be ... Essentially, we would send
it, it would take four years to get there,

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:32.580
and then another four years to come back.
So there would be an eight-year turnaround

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:36.770
time on communication for us to get a reply
when send a message back.

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:40.730
So that makes it very possible that we could
have a little nice back-and-forth, but what

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:45.250
happens if intelligent life is thousands of
years away? Thousands of light-years away.

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:50.540
Yeah, that's when it gets hard, and this is
where METI, the messaging aspect is somewhat

00:15:50.540 --> 00:15:54.660
limited because we've only been producing
radio waves for what? A hundred years, would

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:59.620
you say? Something like that. So only a hundred
light-year bubble around the Earth, actually,

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:03.520
would be aware of our radio transmissions.
Everybody else in the Milky Way, up to a hundred

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:07.120
thousand light-years away, they might be able
to tell there's a habitable planet here, but

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:10.210
they wouldn't know that we were producing
radio waves because there hasn't been enough

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:13.690
time for those radio waves to have reached
them yet. So yeah, certainly when you look

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:19.300
at the grander scale of the Milky Way, it's
a very limited volume of the Milky Way that

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:23.390
would certainly know that we were producing
radio emissions.

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:30.890
So Kathryn, tell us: if we did get a signal
that was from a planet thousands of light-years

00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:33.810
away, how do we know that civilization is
still existing?

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:39.040
We don't. So I mean, that's one of the really
difficult things. So let's assume a signal

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:43.470
does come in, and I think everything that
you've said in terms of the content et cetera

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:47.370
is super important, and they're all sort of
facets of the search is conducted that means

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:50.900
that a signal would be somewhat averaged out,
and so a lot of the content would simply drop

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:56.040
out. So first of all, I think it's important
to realize that content, if it is there, would

00:16:56.040 --> 00:17:02.130
be difficult to decipher.
And so anything that would conceivably tell

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:06.760
us kind of the status of that civilization,
we just really, actually, wouldn't have access

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:12.850
to. So let's say that we do get a signal,
it's only one as opposed ... I mean, observations

00:17:12.850 --> 00:17:16.640
would of course continue to see if there is
a steady stream or something like that, but

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:22.160
let's say that a signal comes in. We would
really have a difficult time, I think, deciding

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:27.920
whether than civilization or that society,
let's use that word instead, perhaps, is extant

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:31.780
or extinct. And then that kind of changes
the equation in some ways. There's been a

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:36.770
couple of great science fiction stories, for
example, about transmissions from an alien

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:44.220
society, but basically they're just transmitting
to say , "Goodbye, we're dying." How does

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:49.710
that make us feel better? I don't know.
Is there anyway that we can study societies

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:54.820
on Earth to know? Like, does society have
a lifespan, and then we just build nuclear

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:58.280
weapons, and eventually kill ourselves?
Right, okay. Great question.

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:02.520
When Carl Sagan was writing about the lifetimes
of technological civilizations, he was very

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:08.280
concerned about our own. So for example, when
Cosmos came out in 1980, he was kind of fighting

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:13.590
a battle on the side at the same time, a really,
really, important one, regarding the effects

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:18.470
of nuclear winter, and the fact that a nuclear
war isn't winnable. So he was very preoccupied

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:25.600
with at that time with this idea of our technological
adolescence. You know, how do we, as a global

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:29.180
society, deal with the fact that we've achieved
the capacity to destroy ourselves? And he

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:34.490
was hoping that if we find a signal from the
great beyond, that if would be proof that

00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:40.710
another civilization has survived this technological
adolescence, and has kind of come out the

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:47.180
other side and that maybe they could teach
us something about how to be a sustainable,

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:51.820
ideally peaceful, civilization.
But the thing is, you know, if we're trying

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:57.710
to understand civilizations out there from
civilizations here ... Personally, my opinion

00:18:57.710 --> 00:19:02.930
as an anthropologist is that: we can speculate
all we like about alien sociology, but it

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:05.240
is speculation.
But we do. But that's what we're here to do.

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:11.640
Well, that's what you're here to do. But actually,
I don't think that we can say anything all

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:17.000
that meaningful. So there's kind of been every
theory put forward about, for a civilization

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:20.700
to last a long time they would have to be
peaceful. Others say, “No, they would just

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:24.050
have to be hyper aggressive and wipe everybody
out.” The problem is is that we don't have

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:26.900
the data to distinguish between those possibilities
at this time.

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:33.480
And so if a detection is achieved, we won't
actually know for sure what that means, and

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:37.440
you'll have kind of every conceivable specialty
on Earth saying, “Well, this means this,

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:42.010
and this means this.” But we won't actually
know. So then that comes into these questions

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:46.840
about, well, what do you want to believe?
And how much do you want to believe it?

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:51.270
That kind of is a nice lead-in to ... Let's
take this alien adventure into phase two.

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:56.210
So say we don't just get a signal, but the
alien spaceship arrives here, and they come

00:19:56.210 --> 00:20:02.840
out. We're going to also shift this conversation
into a couple of gears, but Marcelo, let's

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:08.300
spend some time talk about your research.
You essentially talk to an alien species,

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:10.560
if you will, dolphins.
Yes, sort of.

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:14.810
So after decades of research, because when
these aliens come we're going to want to communicate

00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:19.700
with them. After decades of research, how
well can we communicate with dolphins?

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:28.820
Well, I would say not really that well, at
all. In my particular case, I studied dolphins

00:20:28.820 --> 00:20:36.970
5 years ago thinking that it was assumed that
we would be able to have an understanding

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:41.800
of at least whether or not they have something,
a communication system, approaching the complexity

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:49.150
of human language. And 5 years later, I can
tell you that I still don't have an idea.

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:54.820
It's remarkably difficult to understand all
of these issues. The moment you step away

00:20:54.820 --> 00:21:00.300
from, anthropomorphism, right? I mean, it's
very easy to imagine that you can communicate

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:06.920
with the great apes, which we have so much
in common. But the moment you step into a

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:13.000
completely different physical environment,
like water ... Trying to communicate with

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:19.610
these other animals, okay? There is some extent
to which we understand each other as mammals

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:24.400
all do with one another, and some extent to
which we don't.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:29.720
Can you tell me, perhaps, an example of where
maybe you had your best communication with

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:34.460
a dolphin after 5 years? Where you really
felt, “Yeah, we're getting somewhere here?"

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:46.050
My best communication, okay? So my best communication
was once, we were diving. We were pursuing

00:21:46.050 --> 00:21:53.910
a group. We had a group of teenage dolphins
off of Belize, and they were just lounging

00:21:53.910 --> 00:22:00.090
around and horsing with one another. Some
of them were sort of sleeping, a siesta. It

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:07.880
was 2 pm. And they were all teenagers, if
I recall correctly. They were all male, and

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:10.929
they were just horsing with each other. You
know, teenage style, okay? They were raking

00:22:10.929 --> 00:22:18.090
their teeth on each other and stuff. And there
was a group of these two teenage dolphins

00:22:18.090 --> 00:22:22.890
that were going like that. The essential group
of teenagers at the cafeteria that are always

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:28.010
together going around, and sneaking up to
everybody else. And we had been following

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:31.420
them with helicopter drones, okay? And at
some point the batteries cut out, and I decide:

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:38.059
Okay I'm going to jump in the water with the
GoPro, and record from the water because we

00:22:38.059 --> 00:22:42.140
had been on them for like an hour and a half,
okay? And it was really fascinating, you know,

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:49.410
all this display of behavior.
And I get in the water and there's these two

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:54.360
teenagers that come around. And dolphins always,
when you're in the water, you hear them well

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:58.860
before you can see them.
What sound do they make?

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:04.929
So the sounds that dolphins make are a combination
of two very different sounds. One of them

00:23:04.929 --> 00:23:11.760
is whistle-like, at very high-frequency. It's
sounds like. And a different sound is made

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:18.120
by the echolocation clicks. That sounds like.
And they can pulse that at an extremely fast

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:21.980
pace, okay? And when they do that, it sounds
like a creaking door, maybe. Like this chair

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:30.450
that's creaking, like. Kind of thing. And
so, these two guys were just lounging around,

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:35.710
okay? They come around, and they look at me.
They echolocate at me. They turn, and the

00:23:35.710 --> 00:23:43.670
moment they turn, they start chattering. And
I had the most distinct impression they were

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:49.361
laughing at me.
So that's the one time I understood what they

00:23:49.361 --> 00:23:54.510
were ... I don't know exactly what they were
saying about me, okay? Maybe they didn't like

00:23:54.510 --> 00:24:02.690
my fins, maybe they thought that my figure
in the water was unshapely and gangly, okay?

00:24:02.690 --> 00:24:06.730
Or maybe they were commenting on the fact
that having both eyes on the front of the

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:11.720
face, instead of the sides, makes me look
like a flounder or something. I don't know,

00:24:11.720 --> 00:24:15.730
okay?
Maybe that's what first contact will be like.

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:20.140
They were definitely laughing at me.
We actually have a video of your research.

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:24.929
We have two videos. What if we play ... This
is one of dolphins surfing. Do you want to

00:24:24.929 --> 00:24:29.500
introduce it?
Yes, I think that the video ... So here we

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:35.990
are on a boat, okay? You can see we have two
Gopher balls, and you heard the sounds of

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:41.059
the dolphins as they came about right to the
front of the boat. So what you are seeing

00:24:41.059 --> 00:24:47.190
is the film made from the front of the boat.
Dolphins from, you know, we know from the

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:52.630
that they love the boat ride. They love to
surf on the pressure wave on the front of

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:58.230
the boat, and this is one of the things that
we're trying study.

00:24:58.230 --> 00:25:03.850
We're trying to get them to catch their communication
in a very restricted activity, so that by

00:25:03.850 --> 00:25:08.330
restricting the nature of the activity, we
can correlate what they are doing with their

00:25:08.330 --> 00:25:12.630
emissions. And we have a better chance at
... Otherwise, language is combinatorial and

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:17.960
there is no way can explore all possible combinations
of all possible things, okay? You're never

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:23.960
going to be able to fill up a table with all
the combinations. So what we do, in this case,

00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:29.910
is we try to restrict the kind of behavior
so that the presumably there's a restricted

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:35.179
set of words, or verbs, or whatever it is
that they use.

00:25:35.179 --> 00:25:39.530
So they're unlikely to say, like, “Hey,
let's go to the shore,” if you're in the

00:25:39.530 --> 00:25:43.120
middle of a-
Yeah, so if they are doing acrobatics, and

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:47.640
there is a systematic wave that goes like
every time that one is going to jump over

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:54.210
the other one, we have at least proof that
there's a correlation between a given vocal

00:25:54.210 --> 00:26:01.660
emission and given physical act. Okay? Being
next to the dolphins, we don't believe that

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:07.750
there's any way that we can actually figure
out the language if we cannot correlate behavior.

00:26:07.750 --> 00:26:13.020
That's why I don't think that you would learn
an alien language, at least by listening to

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:16.910
a transmission, okay? Because I mean, you
would have to find error curves and means

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:22.130
of defining how to transmit the dictionary.
Okay? And what I mean by this, and how do

00:26:22.130 --> 00:26:25.410
you refer to physical objects if you don't
have a common frame.

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:31.780
So you talked about this a little bit, but
what does your knowledge about how much we

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:36.240
now understand about how dolphins speak, how
much confidence does that give you that we'll

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:39.280
understand what the aliens tell us?
Hmm

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:46.179
Doesn't look good, eh?
Well, I mean, we live with dolphins. We have

00:26:46.179 --> 00:26:53.120
known dolphins for two thousand years, okay?
I mean, go to the Metropolitan Museum, you'll

00:26:53.120 --> 00:27:02.799
see them all over the Greek wing. You'll see
they were sacred animals in Greece. So I don't

00:27:02.799 --> 00:27:10.860
know. It doesn't give me great confidence.
If the moment that you step away from certain

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:15.299
commonalities ... I mean, the interesting
thing is that dolphins are still mammals,

00:27:15.299 --> 00:27:21.380
recognizably mammals, and they have a certain
... They are able to recognize gestures and

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:26.610
interact with us in ways that I would not
anticipate would necessarily be the case with

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:32.610
an alien species, who might not even be made
out of carbon. It might be some gas cloud

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:37.799
with, you know, entrails and stuff.
Just a walking fart.

00:27:37.799 --> 00:27:44.600
Yeah. So who knows what they are, okay? I
mean, the moment you posit, you know, people

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:51.830
with limbs and stuff, you're really positing
a lot. Right? And it's interesting, dolphins

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:56.630
do not have arms, yet when trainers point
at something, dolphins understand they're

00:27:56.630 --> 00:28:02.220
pointing at something, and they ran to that,
okay? Even though they don't have means of

00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:07.630
pointing with hands. So there's all these
commonalities that come from having a common

00:28:07.630 --> 00:28:14.830
neural substrate. We have brains of an essentially
similar texture, okay? Whatever our engines

00:28:14.830 --> 00:28:21.150
in our brain that powers motion, that powers
attitudes, they come from a common ground,

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:23.770
okay?
And therefore, when we interact together,

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:28.480
we understand gestures. Dolphins are very
gestural. You know, there's a lot of nonvocal

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:33.390
communication between them, in addition to
the vocal communication, which is a lot. So

00:28:33.390 --> 00:28:39.320
I don't know, I mean if you work together
with an alien with which you cannot even understand

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:43.890
physical gestures, where do you start? I have
no idea.

00:28:43.890 --> 00:28:50.520
This takes us, what a beautiful segue, Karen,
to the linguist. Where do we start? You are

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:56.780
our Amy Adams, sent to the aliens that've
come forth. And they say, "Karen, just sort

00:28:56.780 --> 00:29:01.443
it out." What are they saying? What other
steps? Walk me through what would you do?

00:29:01.443 --> 00:29:05.040
What are you gonna do?
Well that was great ... I don't know. I don't

00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:12.960
know how much we could do. In one sense, the
stuff Marcelo brought up is very relevant.

00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:17.140
Because so now in this scenario I'm faced
with aliens, so I can get a little bit of

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:23.470
sense of what ... Do they resemble something
like a mammal? So, in one sense, chances are

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:28.750
the aliens share no kind of similarity to
our brain architecture, to our neuro-architecture.

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:33.320
So I can't assume that the way they process
the world is the same as the way I process

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:36.450
the world, or they way a dolphin processes
the world. But I could at least maybe see

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:41.100
if they have something that looks kind of
like arms, or legs, or sense whether they

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:44.270
have vision.
So the first thing I think I would do, and

00:29:44.270 --> 00:29:48.210
I should say I'm not a field linguist, so
I don't ... I'm not like Amy Adams in the

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:50.910
movie, a field linguist, I think, and she
goes out into the field. Those are people

00:29:50.910 --> 00:29:55.890
who actually go out and talk to people, and
try to figure out how foreign languages work.

00:29:55.890 --> 00:30:00.450
But I think one thing that I would do, and
one thing that I don't think is highlighted

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:08.360
very often is just figure out what their physical
senses are. Like, can they see? Do they see

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:13.010
things in roughly the same way? Do they process
vision in roughly the same way we do? Can

00:30:13.010 --> 00:30:19.040
they hear? At what frequency can they hear?
What kind of sounds, or gestures, or other

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:22.870
signals are they emitting? To get a sense
of what kind of modality I'm even going to

00:30:22.870 --> 00:30:26.960
try to communicate with them.
So to go back to Arrival, Amy Adams decides

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:32.100
to start writing in English, which is not,
I think, how I would start, by writing on

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:35.860
a whiteboard in English. Because that might
be one of the hardest ways for them to figure

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:41.670
out what I was saying to them. And I think
at some point, I don't know if I'm remember

00:30:41.670 --> 00:30:44.500
this right, but she starts to teach them one
of the first things. It's something like her

00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:49.561
name, and then that she's walking, which is
a really weird thing to start with. Maybe

00:30:49.561 --> 00:30:53.980
not the name, but you have to assume that
they have a practice of naming, too, and that

00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:58.340
they're going to understand what you're doing.
And then, these animals don't walk at all,

00:30:58.340 --> 00:31:01.940
that she's dealing with in the movie. They're
kind of cephalopods that move through this

00:31:01.940 --> 00:31:06.530
weird kind of substance, like this.
They did walk, though.

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:09.490
They kind of walk. They kind of like-
Shuffle.

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:10.700
Shuffle.
Yeah.

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:14.340
So I'm not sure. This is just to highlight
that it's not clear what kind of concepts

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:18.890
they would have. So it's not clear where you
can start, but I think starting by seeing

00:31:18.890 --> 00:31:24.789
how they respond to the environment. Just
taking data on sort of what sounds, or images,

00:31:24.789 --> 00:31:31.179
or other kind of data they emit under what
certain stimuli would be the place to start.

00:31:31.179 --> 00:31:35.130
Can you give me an example?
Can I give you an example? Yeah. Sure. So

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:42.950
just very simply, the idea would be suppose
I take an object like a red square, and I

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:47.380
put it in front of them. And then I see if
they say anything. Or if they pick it up,

00:31:47.380 --> 00:31:54.211
what do they say? And then I can maybe vary
that, like try a red ball. And then, if something

00:31:54.211 --> 00:31:57.460
is in common ... I mean, this is a very toy
example because it would take a lot more data

00:31:57.460 --> 00:32:01.179
than this. But if they something in common,
maybe they're picking up on red. And then

00:32:01.179 --> 00:32:05.390
pick at something else that's the same shape
as the cube, but a different color. And if

00:32:05.390 --> 00:32:08.760
there's something in common that they emitted
with the first thing, then maybe they were

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:13.230
talking about the shape. So maybe this gives
me some indication of what their language

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:16.450
is like, and some indication of if they're
picking up on color, or shape, or something

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:19.559
like that.
Tell me about this idea that David had suggested

00:32:19.559 --> 00:32:22.000
with using math. Prime numbers, was the example.
Yeah.

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:25.760
What do you think of this idea? What if we
just started, you know-

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:29.510
So there's two things to think about when
using math. So some people talk about ... His

00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:35.361
idea of using just a sequence of prime numbers,
I think for that, the question is: what do

00:32:35.361 --> 00:32:40.040
you want to do in communicating? Now, if you
want to just indicate that there's intelligent

00:32:40.040 --> 00:32:44.760
life here, then I think that might be a good
way to go. Alright, because if it's true that

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:49.630
prime numbers don't occur in nature, you might
be showing: here's some sort of signal that

00:32:49.630 --> 00:32:53.180
there's something non-natural here, something
that had to be produced by an intelligent

00:32:53.180 --> 00:32:57.520
species.
They're not going to be in particularly impressed,

00:32:57.520 --> 00:33:00.160
are they? They're not going to be like, "Whoa,
they're sending me prime numbers, these humans!"

00:33:00.160 --> 00:33:03.710
Yeah, but if you want to do more than just
say, “Hey, we're here,” if you want to

00:33:03.710 --> 00:33:09.940
communicate, then you might have to go beyond
math, or beyond at least simple sequences

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:13.780
of things like prime numbers.
The second thing, I think, to keep in mind,

00:33:13.780 --> 00:33:19.340
is some people talk about math as this universal
language. But math, itself, is not a language.

00:33:19.340 --> 00:33:23.960
When we're talking about mathematical concepts,
they still have to be encoded in some sort

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:27.450
of symbolic system. That is, they're still
going to be these units of meaning that are

00:33:27.450 --> 00:33:31.010
somewhat arbitrary that match on to the concepts.
It won't be completely arbitrary, like in

00:33:31.010 --> 00:33:35.039
the sense of representing numbers, we can
do one beep for one, two beeps for two, or

00:33:35.039 --> 00:33:38.630
something like that. But once you get beyond
the numbers, if we want to encode addition,

00:33:38.630 --> 00:33:43.360
let's say, we encode it in our mathematical
system by writing a little plus. But that's

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:48.900
just one of the many ways we could arbitrarily
encode addition, or something like that. And

00:33:48.900 --> 00:33:53.150
they're still going to have to decode it.
Or if they send a message to us about math,

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:58.180
we're still going to have to decode it.
And there's still going to be a syntax. Our

00:33:58.180 --> 00:34:01.169
writing system for math, if we want to write
two plus two is four, first we write the two,

00:34:01.169 --> 00:34:05.110
then we put the plus in the middle, then we
put the two, equals and four. But there's

00:34:05.110 --> 00:34:09.179
systems, like in computer programming languages,
where you might put the plus up front. Right?

00:34:09.179 --> 00:34:13.370
That's just a very simple system. And so,
there's still a syntax, there's still a grammar

00:34:13.370 --> 00:34:16.800
to math, and there's still an encoding, and
someone's still going to have to figure that

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:21.919
out. So I think it's solves some of the challenges,
because I think there is good reason to think

00:34:21.919 --> 00:34:26.300
that any ... Especially if the aliens have
arrived on Earth, that they have enough understanding

00:34:26.300 --> 00:34:31.030
of mathematics to have built a spaceship,
and come here. And so we still have some of

00:34:31.030 --> 00:34:35.409
the systems because maybe that's the one place
where we share some concepts. It could solve

00:34:35.409 --> 00:34:38.159
some of the problems, rather, because that's
one place where we share some concepts, but

00:34:38.159 --> 00:34:43.419
we haven't solved sort of many of the layers.
If I could just add to that a little bit,

00:34:43.419 --> 00:34:47.830
it actually touches on the METI, as well,
the messaging. Because when the first messages

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:52.210
were being designed, and Carl Sagan helped
design some of the messages, one example is

00:34:52.210 --> 00:34:57.690
the Arecibo Message that was very famously
broadcast from Puerto Rico, this giant Arecibo

00:34:57.690 --> 00:35:03.690
array. And another was these gold disks, and
carvings that were put on the Voyager spacecraft

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:09.050
that Carl Sagan also had a hand in putting
on. And on them, there is a little bit of

00:35:09.050 --> 00:35:14.050
maths, and they're often designed to try to
teach you the syntax. So it gives several

00:35:14.050 --> 00:35:17.060
examples of two, two, four, and it doesn't
really matter what the symbols are. If they

00:35:17.060 --> 00:35:22.040
see enough examples, hopefully they can figure
out what the syntax is. And then also just

00:35:22.040 --> 00:35:27.370
diagrams. This what a human being looks like,
this is the local pulsars around us so you

00:35:27.370 --> 00:35:32.440
can locate exactly where the Earth is, for
instance. That was on these original messages.

00:35:32.440 --> 00:35:35.910
The downside of that is, though, with gold
disk it's kind of ... You just hold it, and

00:35:35.910 --> 00:35:40.580
you can kind of just look at it, and there's
no way to decode it. You're just sort of really

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:46.730
studying the content. The Arecibo Message
was a 23-by-73 pixel image. But if they didn't

00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:52.170
know it was that way around, if they did 73-by-23
instead, you just get garbage. So you have

00:35:52.170 --> 00:35:57.859
to know the key to construct the image.
So a visual representation seems appealing

00:35:57.859 --> 00:36:01.619
because you can teach them all of our maths,
and what a human being looks like, but if

00:36:01.619 --> 00:36:05.490
they don't know the correct key, they might
unlock it and it looks like garbage to us,

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:08.840
and they're like, “Oh, that looks like something
I recognize.” And they might think that's

00:36:08.840 --> 00:36:12.080
the correct signal. But if they don't know
in advance what they key is, then they're

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:17.280
kind of stuck.
May I add that I think that it's a huge assumption

00:36:17.280 --> 00:36:24.470
to think that the aliens think that 1 plus
1 is the beginning of math. Okay? That we

00:36:24.470 --> 00:36:30.840
are based on whatever, beings on sort of scale,
energy scale. We have fingers, we count on

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:36.640
our fingers. We start with counting, okay?
If we have an intelligence who operated in

00:36:36.640 --> 00:36:42.060
the quantum mechanical regime, quantum computing
is fundamentally distinct from our mathematic

00:36:42.060 --> 00:36:48.380
computing. And they might not have the notion
of objects as, you know, counting objects

00:36:48.380 --> 00:36:58.800
as their primary method of counting. So it
might not be that an alien civilization considers

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:08.360
one, two, three, five, seven, eleven, to be
proof of intelligence anyway. Okay? So even

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:18.500
at this level, if you do not put a bound on
which kind of life you're dealing with, then

00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:22.590
you cannot conceivably put a bound on how
we are going to communicate with it.

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:28.660
I think you're right, that in a way, you kind
of have no choice but to put a bound. Because

00:37:28.660 --> 00:37:31.070
otherwise
sufficiently similar to us.

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:37.099
So when you take a baby, and you put them
in any culture, they can learn the language.

00:37:37.099 --> 00:37:41.820
So should we just give the aliens a baby,
and then wait for it to grow up?

00:37:41.820 --> 00:37:50.910
Let's check with the ethics board.
Child services may be called

00:37:50.910 --> 00:37:54.990
Could we just make a baby live amongst the
dolphins, and then could we then get it to

00:37:54.990 --> 00:38:03.109
decipher what's going on?
Yeah. That's going to work.

00:38:03.109 --> 00:38:08.220
if we were to discover and contact intelligent
life, it would be one of the biggest moments

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:15.680
of human history, if not the biggest, how
do you think it would go over on Earth? Kathryn,

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:21.380
what does happen next?
It depends. As an anthropologist, that's the

00:38:21.380 --> 00:38:27.619
only answer I can give. So it depends on what's
happening in the world at that time, it depends

00:38:27.619 --> 00:38:31.030
on who you are.
Say, right now. Say, tomorrow.

00:38:31.030 --> 00:38:33.849
Tomorrow, what time?
9:30.

00:38:33.849 --> 00:38:39.480
Early? 9:30?
Yeah, commute. Everyone's coming to work.

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:42.740
Commute? Okay, alright. How does the detection
unfold?

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:46.359
Twitter.
Twitter, okay. Alright. So we got Twitter,

00:38:46.359 --> 00:38:51.080
we got 9:30. Who's it coming from?
The aliens.

00:38:51.080 --> 00:38:58.290
Oh, it's coming straight from the aliens?
Do you guys want to participate in this hypothetical?

00:38:58.290 --> 00:39:02.790
Awesome, okay. Now this we can have fun with,
okay. So the aliens have hacked Twitter.

00:39:02.790 --> 00:39:06.910
Say it comes from the SETI Twitter account.
Okay, say it's coming from one of the SETI

00:39:06.910 --> 00:39:10.060
Twitter accounts, okay. Found it, “Hi.”
Okay.

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:17.290
Yeah, little alien emoji.
Right. So I think, you know, here's the really

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:20.540
interesting thing, then. I think we're going
to have a whole lot of human questions at

00:39:20.540 --> 00:39:25.220
that moment, right? So like, maybe in the
Q and A, we can talk about what your questions

00:39:25.220 --> 00:39:30.470
would be at that moment. Like what would you
want to know? So there are a lot of really

00:39:30.470 --> 00:39:36.220
important things that would affect the reception
of that signal. First of all, let's imagine

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:43.740
you're in North Korea, and you've just heard
this news from the U.S. What's your reaction?

00:39:43.740 --> 00:39:49.599
Other places in the world, similarly. So would
they accept, say, the authority of a scientist

00:39:49.599 --> 00:39:53.210
coming out of the U.S. about, you know, we
really did find this thing.

00:39:53.210 --> 00:39:56.190
Given all the atmosphere around fake news
and all that, I think there could be a lot

00:39:56.190 --> 00:40:01.660
of distrust, a lot of confusion, and a lot
of uncertainty about who to trust, let's say.

00:40:01.660 --> 00:40:06.180
I should specify here, I'm Canadian. So let's
say that the president of the United States

00:40:06.180 --> 00:40:09.570
decides to get in the Twitter theme.
Kind of hard to-

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:13.650
I know, it's really unheard of, but like,
what happens then? So does that contribute,

00:40:13.650 --> 00:40:18.180
or detract? You know, it certainly messes
things up creatively, and interestingly. Then,

00:40:18.180 --> 00:40:21.670
you know, there's the effects on the stock
market. So people are kind of going, “Okay,

00:40:21.670 --> 00:40:27.570
what doe we want to invest in? What do we
want to divest from? Okay, weapons, pharmaceuticals,

00:40:27.570 --> 00:40:32.240
prepping equipment.” You know. You can kind
of easily imagine things like that. From the

00:40:32.240 --> 00:40:38.850
surveys that have been done on people's anticipations
about extraterrestrial life, et cetera, some

00:40:38.850 --> 00:40:42.650
people are very anxious about it. Some people
are very excited. Some people are like, “Beam

00:40:42.650 --> 00:40:47.720
me up, please.”
Or like, “No, no, no, I'm heading for the

00:40:47.720 --> 00:40:54.150
hills.” So there could be a lot of different
responses, and I think when I said it depends,

00:40:54.150 --> 00:40:58.980
it depends also on what else is happening
in the world. So if you're in a war zone already,

00:40:58.980 --> 00:41:03.300
if you're recently recovering from a war,
if you been traumatized in any way, this might

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:06.920
really be the last thing that you want. If,
on the other hand, you're just kind of very

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:13.100
hopeful about what aliens might provide to
human civilization, you know ... And there's

00:41:13.100 --> 00:41:21.120
also, from the surveys that have been done,
there's a real kind of difference in degree

00:41:21.120 --> 00:41:25.540
of likelihood to believe. So there are regional
differences, there are political differences,

00:41:25.540 --> 00:41:28.920
there are religious differences.
Can you be more specific? So who is more likely

00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:32.520
to believe?
Well. So there's also questions about, you

00:41:32.520 --> 00:41:36.300
know governmental authority, and how people
are more likely to believe in conspiracies,

00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:40.380
et cetera. So I mean, there is differences
between the West, the Midwest, the Northeast.

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:44.330
There's male/female differences.
Wait, wait. So if there was a sign, who is

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:48.630
more ... Like the West, the East? Like Californians
versus New York, like a little more specific.

00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:52.680
I don't have the statistics right in my head,
because I wasn't the one who did the research.

00:41:52.680 --> 00:41:55.560
I think the point I'm just trying to make
is that there is variation, and then that's

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:59.119
going to kind of affect kind of the overall
reception. And then there's the question of,

00:41:59.119 --> 00:42:02.020
“Oh my goodness, well, what do we do?”
Because, again, some people are very much

00:42:02.020 --> 00:42:07.340
in favor of sending a reply, and whether or
not there's a global response that's says,

00:42:07.340 --> 00:42:12.750
“Yes, let's write a message together, and
send it.” You know somebody is going to.

00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:16.790
It's just going to happen. And then other
people will be upset, because it'll be like,

00:42:16.790 --> 00:42:23.110
“I wish we had just stayed quiet.” And
I think there could be a scope for quite a

00:42:23.110 --> 00:42:28.900
lot of friction here. So the research that
has been done on potential receptions to contact,

00:42:28.900 --> 00:42:35.240
it indicates a lot of instability. So I think
one frame that SETI is understood within is

00:42:35.240 --> 00:42:40.210
just the idea that this would just be a continuation
of all the research that has indicated that

00:42:40.210 --> 00:42:46.440
we're not the center of the universe. So we
are just on a planet, orbiting a sun, among

00:42:46.440 --> 00:42:52.109
many planets orbiting many suns. So that's
been the huge contribution of astronomy over

00:42:52.109 --> 00:42:55.800
the last few centuries, has been kind of de-centering
us, right?

00:42:55.800 --> 00:42:59.599
So that's one frame of understanding. But
another is that, okay well, these are other

00:42:59.599 --> 00:43:05.220
sentient beings, it's not just about our place.
So then it all becomes about, well, what's

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:09.290
the relationship going to be like? And how
does that play into existing relationships

00:43:09.290 --> 00:43:13.170
here on Earth?
So Marcelo, as someone who doesn't research

00:43:13.170 --> 00:43:19.770
this specific question directly, as a regular
civilian but rational thinker, how would you

00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:23.230
feel?
Well, first of all, the most important thing

00:43:23.230 --> 00:43:28.600
is to know what kind of signal we got. Namely,
if we got a signal from two thousand light-years

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:33.720
away, okay, and these guys might not even
be alive by now, it's very different than

00:43:33.720 --> 00:43:39.710
if you have a huge UFO fly into Central Park.
Yeah.

00:43:39.710 --> 00:43:48.450
So it's obviously a different thing. In every
single science fiction movie which you have

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:57.119
an arrival of a hostile civilization, their
UFOs are creepy. And whenever a friendly civilization

00:43:57.119 --> 00:44:06.700
comes, the UFOs are cute. And this is absurd,
okay? Namely, that there is no basis for thinking

00:44:06.700 --> 00:44:12.740
that we have a common aesthetic code, or that
they would make any devices for transport

00:44:12.740 --> 00:44:18.460
according to our preferences of what is creepy
or cute, right? So all of the sudden, here

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:27.359
comes this huge, enormously creepy UFO, and
the guy says to us, “Hey, hello, how are

00:44:27.359 --> 00:44:33.119
you?” Right? We don't know. We're making
this whole thing up.

00:44:33.119 --> 00:44:40.680
There is actually almost a dress rehearsal
of exactly what happened, though. A real example

00:44:40.680 --> 00:44:45.950
of this, which is the War of the Worlds broadcast
in 1938. So that was radio broadcast by Columbia

00:44:45.950 --> 00:44:54.550
Broadcasting Systems, and it's H.G. Wells'
famous story that was made for radio with

00:44:54.550 --> 00:44:59.310
a narrator. And it's done in a way that's
like a news report, if you listen to it. So

00:44:59.310 --> 00:45:04.010
people who just tuned in halfway through really
thought it was an invasion, because it sounds

00:45:04.010 --> 00:45:08.230
... There's like journalist on the scene during
the moments when the aliens come out of the

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:12.560
spaceship, and stuff, and start lasering everyone.
So people were pretty panicked about it, and

00:45:12.560 --> 00:45:17.010
actually the telephone switchboard of the
broadcaster got completely inundated with

00:45:17.010 --> 00:45:23.300
phone calls of concerned people. Police arrived
at the radio station, and then there was journalistic

00:45:23.300 --> 00:45:27.530
reports the next day. And The New York Times
had a headline about, you know, people confused

00:45:27.530 --> 00:45:32.730
thinking their radio broadcast was real.
So that was kind of like a one of the few

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:38.610
insights, although that wasn't really contact,
that was a hostile invasion. So that was not

00:45:38.610 --> 00:45:43.380
surprising that people panicked, in that case.
But it's one of the few historical incidences

00:45:43.380 --> 00:45:47.490
where we have some insight as to how people
might react thinking it was a genuine event.

00:45:47.490 --> 00:45:53.099
That's an interesting one, too, because it
kind of spun out into this whole new kind

00:45:53.099 --> 00:45:57.660
of distrust in the popular media. Because
the feeling was: well, if you can pull that

00:45:57.660 --> 00:46:03.050
on us, that's not cool. So it actually started
an interesting trend. But there's also been

00:46:03.050 --> 00:46:06.760
a lot reanalysis of that one, about you know
just exactly how much people were panicked,

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:10.780
et cetera. But there've also been echoes.
So there was a case, for example, a few years

00:46:10.780 --> 00:46:16.760
later in Quito, where the same thing was rebroadcast.
Unfortunately, it went really, really badly

00:46:16.760 --> 00:46:22.849
in that particular case, and it was because
they'd only recently come out of a war themselves,

00:46:22.849 --> 00:46:28.410
horrific invasions, et cetera. And the people
who had fought defensively in that war, didn't

00:46:28.410 --> 00:46:34.230
think this was funny. They torched the radio
station, people died. You know? And that doesn't

00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:37.849
get cited, but that's something that I think
of, for example, when I kind of think about

00:46:37.849 --> 00:46:43.080
potential negative consequences.
Just a question for everyone on the panel,

00:46:43.080 --> 00:46:47.670
what do you think of this idea that if we
know there's alien life out there, we'll all

00:46:47.670 --> 00:46:53.720
forget our differences, and like save racism
for the aliens?

00:46:53.720 --> 00:46:59.540
That's the usual kind of hopeful take on things.
It's part of the kind of Sagan-esque justification

00:46:59.540 --> 00:47:05.140
for shifting our frame of reference, and for
... And when I say justification, that sounds

00:47:05.140 --> 00:47:11.310
a little bit harsh. It's the cultural frame
of reference that says by having that confirmation,

00:47:11.310 --> 00:47:17.170
that again, another society has survived their
technological adolescence, that it could bring

00:47:17.170 --> 00:47:25.810
out the best in us, somehow. From what I understand
of world history, I don't really buy it. I

00:47:25.810 --> 00:47:30.680
think it has just as much potential to bring
out divisions, especially if there is actually

00:47:30.680 --> 00:47:34.839
some degree of actual contact.
I don't think it would be surprising to see

00:47:34.839 --> 00:47:38.740
jockeying, because this is something that
we've seen in real contact episodes on Earth.

00:47:38.740 --> 00:47:42.470
It's not just, you know, one side and the
other side. It's like, “How can I make this

00:47:42.470 --> 00:47:46.310
work to my advantage?” And so, it's people
trying to build alliances, and then it becomes

00:47:46.310 --> 00:47:49.950
my enemies enemy is my friend. It gets very
complicated, it's doesn't just kind of come

00:47:49.950 --> 00:47:55.849
down into, “Oh, we're all suddenly unified
in humanity, holding hands, singing Kumbaya,”

00:47:55.849 --> 00:48:00.839
et cetera. I think it's likely to be more
complicated than that, and not necessarily

00:48:00.839 --> 00:48:06.109
all so adorable, alas.
I was going to jump in. I agree. I think it's

00:48:06.109 --> 00:48:09.260
also just very unlikely that, even in the
presence of a new “other,” that's perhaps

00:48:09.260 --> 00:48:13.610
way more other than any of the ones we have
on Earth, like the alien being the new “other,”

00:48:13.610 --> 00:48:19.720
it's unlikely that we're going to overcome
thousands of years of racism, and xenophobia,

00:48:19.720 --> 00:48:24.430
and notice our commonalities in those moments,
rather than, perhaps, those differences being

00:48:24.430 --> 00:48:30.800
highlighted by the fact that we now have to
deal with some sort of eminent, potentially,

00:48:30.800 --> 00:48:39.960
harmful event. This is really, highly speculative,
maybe or if the aliens remained interacting

00:48:39.960 --> 00:48:45.970
with people of Earth over a long, long period
of time, they would be so different that there

00:48:45.970 --> 00:48:50.061
would be more alliances between Earth people
and they would be the new “other,” but

00:48:50.061 --> 00:48:53.020
I don't think that would be anything like
an instant reaction. And I don't even think

00:48:53.020 --> 00:48:56.440
that would necessarily be a likely thing to
happen over a long period of time.

00:48:56.440 --> 00:49:01.340
David?
Well, I just often say to my class that you

00:49:01.340 --> 00:49:06.160
could travel for a billion light-years in
any direction in the universe, and you will

00:49:06.160 --> 00:49:11.750
not meet another human being. That every human
being on this planet is extremely rare, and

00:49:11.750 --> 00:49:15.650
precious in the context of the entire universe.
Even though it seems like there is a lot of

00:49:15.650 --> 00:49:21.349
us, there's like maybe 8 billion, or 7 billion
people right now on the planet, if you divide

00:49:21.349 --> 00:49:26.670
that by the size of the universe, there's
very, very few of us. So I think just even

00:49:26.670 --> 00:49:33.760
forgetting aliens exist, or not, if you just
take a cosmic perspective, that you would

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:38.070
maybe think of your fellow human being as
being a bit more worthy of your kindness and

00:49:38.070 --> 00:49:43.619
compassion, then if you just had this us versus
them attitude. How's it going to react when

00:49:43.619 --> 00:49:49.860
the aliens come? So I think it's almost ... The
better approach is just to think about the

00:49:49.860 --> 00:49:54.920
vast, vast scale of the universe, rather than
to worry about interspecies relations.

00:49:54.920 --> 00:50:00.320
So if we haven't had the feeling of warmth
already, then forget it. We're done.

00:50:00.320 --> 00:50:05.890
Yeah.
Is that the bottom line? Marcelo?

00:50:05.890 --> 00:50:12.690
It's a tough one, right? Basically, everybody
goes “aw,” when they look at dolphins.

00:50:12.690 --> 00:50:18.450
So maybe there's hope for aliens unifying
us, but of course, it depends on which guise

00:50:18.450 --> 00:50:22.670
they come, right? If they're hostile, it's
okay, they see immediately an exasperation

00:50:22.670 --> 00:50:27.020
of all of your divisions. So and so is collaborating
with them. Okay? Or they are going to treason,

00:50:27.020 --> 00:50:39.620
or this, or that, and that's going to be inflamed
in no time flat, right? So I don't know.

00:50:39.620 --> 00:50:45.180
David, in the popular imagination, and even
in sort of scientific thinking, now, the pendulum

00:50:45.180 --> 00:50:50.109
has sort of switched with just with the numbers
game. That there is probably intelligent life

00:50:50.109 --> 00:50:53.900
out there, somewhere. Whether we would ever
communicate with it, we don't know, but it

00:50:53.900 --> 00:50:59.849
seems like the scientific pendulum has switched.
It's probably out there. What if it is not

00:50:59.849 --> 00:51:05.460
out there? What if we are alone? How does
that change your thinking?

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:13.140
Yeah. So I'd just first say that I personally
challenge the idea that the numbers game proves

00:51:13.140 --> 00:51:16.960
that therefore there is intelligence everywhere.
I mean, this kind of goes back to the very

00:51:16.960 --> 00:51:22.520
beginning of the show, but there's no reason
why the probability couldn't be very, very

00:51:22.520 --> 00:51:27.020
tiny. People will often cite the fact that
life emerged early on the Earth as proof of

00:51:27.020 --> 00:51:31.380
the fact that, therefore, life must start
very easily. But it took four billion years

00:51:31.380 --> 00:51:35.070
of evolution to get to this point, and in
a billion years the Sun will make the Earth

00:51:35.070 --> 00:51:39.890
uninhabitable. So it kind of had to have started
pretty soon, else we wouldn't be here to observe

00:51:39.890 --> 00:51:44.780
it. So it's a bit of a circular argument.
So you really can't take the early start to

00:51:44.780 --> 00:51:49.020
life on Earth as meaning anything.
So to answer your question more directly,

00:51:49.020 --> 00:51:54.829
if it does indeed turn out that life is incredibly
precious, then I guess that just really reemphasizes

00:51:54.829 --> 00:52:00.160
my original point about how precious human
beings are on this Earth, that there is obviously

00:52:00.160 --> 00:52:05.390
a set of circumstances which happened here
in this solar system which makes it extremely

00:52:05.390 --> 00:52:11.720
special in a cosmic perspective. I would still
be pretty skeptical that forever, throughout

00:52:11.720 --> 00:52:17.310
the entire history of the universe, there
will be no life elsewhere in the universe.

00:52:17.310 --> 00:52:23.400
I leave intelligence aside, and just say life
elsewhere. And the reason is because in ten

00:52:23.400 --> 00:52:27.640
trillion years there will still be some stars
which are burning hydrogen, and will still

00:52:27.640 --> 00:52:31.831
have planets around them in the right distance
for life. In ten trillion years. And the universe

00:52:31.831 --> 00:52:35.931
is only about ten billion years old. So we're
at the very beginning of the story of the

00:52:35.931 --> 00:52:41.500
universe, still. And actually, most stars
are these small-end dwarfs, which will still

00:52:41.500 --> 00:52:46.630
be burning into the far distant future. So
I think maybe we're just, if we happen to

00:52:46.630 --> 00:52:51.000
be alone right now, it probably means we're
just one of the first, rather than we're necessarily

00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:54.890
gonna be the only ones that will ever, ever
exist. I'd probably take that perspective,

00:52:54.890 --> 00:52:58.840
a little bit more. I think the idea that the
fact that we're alone right now proves that's

00:52:58.840 --> 00:53:03.820
it, we're done, we're the only ones that will
ever be, I wouldn't quite buy that argument.

00:53:03.820 --> 00:53:07.970
So there's plenty of time left in the age
of the universe for other things to happen.

